Hi [Manager Name],
As part of water-source planning for the new electrolysis facility, I pulled =
the Illinois EPA Source Water Assessment Program (SWAP) factsheet data for th=
e 8 water systems we discussed: Farmer City, Springerton, Bartlett, Enfield, =
Crossville, Weldon, Norris City and Waynesville. I then screened every well a=
gainst our three required criteria. The workbook is attached (IL_EPA_SWAP_Wel=
l_Screening.xlsx).

RESULTS AT A GLANCE
- 33 wells were extracted across the 8 systems.
- 7 wells meet all three criteria: depth 160-200 ft, Sand & Gravel aquifer, a=
nd an active description (no abandoned/inactive/disconnected/emergency/sealed=
).
- All 7 are in three DeWitt County systems: Farmer City (4), Weldon (2) and W=
aynesville (1).
- No wells qualify in Springerton, Bartlett, Enfield, Crossville or Norris Ci=
ty. Springerton, Enfield and Crossville list only abandoned wells, and all th=
ree report 100% purchased water. Bartlett's wells are sealed, abandoned, emer=
gency or disconnected, and most are bedrock. Norris City's two active wells a=
re too shallow (97 and 124 ft).

TOP RECOMMENDED OPTIONS (HIGHLIGHTED IN THE ATTACHMENT)
1. Farmer City Well 9 (WL47648) - 188 ft, Sand & Gravel (code 0101), Status A=
, highest reported pumpage of all candidates (26,478,500). It is a named curr=
ent supply well.
2. Farmer City Well 7 (WL47646) - 180 ft, Sand & Gravel (0101), Status A, rep=
orted pumpage 21,976,200. Note: it is active in the IEPA well table but not n=
amed in the system narrative, so its current use needs to be confirmed.
3. Weldon Well 3 (WL45047) - 167 ft, Sand & Gravel (0909), Status A, reported=
 pumpage 5,500,000. It is a named supply well.

OTHER WELLS THAT MEET THE CRITERIA (SECONDARY OPTIONS)
4. Waynesville Well 6 (WL40006): 162 ft, Sand & Gravel, pumpage 4,319,500. It=
 supplies about one quarter of a small 27,000 gal/day system.
5. Weldon Well 4 (WL45048): 163 ft, Sand & Gravel, pumpage 3,503,040.
6-7. Farmer City Well 11 (WL01130, 192 ft) and Well 10 (WL47647, 181 ft): bot=
h meet the criteria, but IEPA reports 0 pumpage, so we have no production his=
tory to rely on.

WHY FARMER CITY IS MY LEAD RECOMMENDATION
Farmer City has the most qualifying wells (4) and the two highest-pumping can=
didates. That points to a productive shallow Sand & Gravel aquifer at the 160=
-200 ft interval and gives us redundancy across multiple wells. Its system av=
erages 257,000 gal/day (per the IEPA narrative).
For scale, DOE puts direct electrolysis water use at about 2.4-8 gal per kg H=
2, depending mostly on cooling design (stoichiometric minimum 2.36 gal/kg). E=
ach 1 metric ton/day of H2 would therefore need roughly 2,400-8,000 gal/day b=
efore pretreatment (RO/DI) reject losses. This is illustrative only. We have =
not fixed the facility's capacity or cooling design, and the actual demand sh=
ould be sized against them.

CAVEATS / OPEN ITEMS
- Pumpage units and reporting period are not stated on the factsheet. The val=
ues are consistent with annual gallons, but I have not confirmed this, so ple=
ase do not quote them as capacity.
- The factsheet narratives for Farmer City and Weldon conflict with the well =
tables (e.g., Farmer City lists Well 2 as a supply well, but the table shows =
it ABANDONED). I screened on the well tables. This needs confirmation with th=
e operators.
- Aquifer codes and the A/I status codes are not defined on the factsheet. I =
did not use Status as a screening rule; all 7 passing wells are Status A.
- These are municipal community supply wells. The realistic paths are a water=
 purchase agreement with the village, or a new project well in the same aquif=
er near the qualifying wells. Either path needs permitting review and water q=
uality data (TDS/hardness for electrolyzer feed), and neither is in the SWAP =
data.
- Near-miss: Waynesville Well 8 (WL40005) is active Sand & Gravel at 207 ft, =
just outside the 200 ft limit. It is the village's primary well (~75 gpm). I =
excluded it under the stated rules but flagged it in case we have flexibility=
 on depth.

PROPOSED NEXT STEPS
1. Contact Farmer City (and Weldon as backup) water operators to confirm well=
 status, available capacity and interest in a supply agreement.
2. Request the full Source Water Assessment reports and recent water quality =
data from the systems. The public factsheets omit location data under IEPA se=
curity policy.
3. Engage our hydrogeology consultant on aquifer yield near Farmer City / Wel=
don, and check permitting requirements for a dedicated project well.

Happy to walk through the workbook. Tab 1 ('All Wells') has a filter on each =
criterion and a 'MEETS ALL CRITERIA' column. Tab 2 ('Potential Wells') lists =
only the 7 qualifying wells, ranked.
Best regards,
[Your Name]
Senior Project Manager

